"","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working out how to decide under A-FDT, treating the two copies of the same model as running fdt on different observations, with symmetric payoffs. I'm weighing how intervening on my output leaves the other copy's input fixed, and how the essay's partial-similarity proposal ties its output to mine with probability r, even though the inputs differ only in incidental details.\n\nI'm checking whether the two copies' inputs make them the same logical node, since the essay's partial-similarity mixture only covers whether the other system computes my function, not what input it gets. Treating them as distinct nodes with the other held at its prior, defect beats cooperate in the prisoner's dilemma by a margin of one or two years. Still, I'm weighing whether the irrelevant differences in input should be ignored, which would change the result.\n\nI'm now looking at the strict A-FDT reading: the other copy is on a different observation, so my intervention leaves its node at prior, and defect dominates. But the essay's red/blue example treats copies on different inputs as separate nodes at prior, which seems directly analogous, so I'm leaning toward submitting the letter.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out how to decide under A-FDT, treating the two copies of the same model as running fdt on different observations, with symmetric payoffs. I'm weighing how intervening on my output leaves the other copy's input fixed, and how the essay's partial-similarity proposal ties its output to mine with probability r, even though the inputs differ only in incidental details.\n\nI'm checking whether the two copies' inputs make them the same logical node, since the essay's partial-similarity mixture only covers whether the other system computes my function, not what input it gets. Treating them as distinct nodes with the other held at its prior, defect beats cooperate in the prisoner's dilemma by a margin of one or two years. Still, I'm weighing whether the irrelevant differences in input should be ignored, which would change the result.\n\nI'm now looking at the strict A-FDT reading: the other copy is on a different observation, so my intervention leaves its node at prior, and defect dominates. But the essay's red/blue example treats copies on different inputs as separate nodes at prior, which seems directly analogous, so I'm leaning toward submitting the letter.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9977,"completion_tokens":1439,"total_tokens":11416,"cost":0.068688,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.068688,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039908,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02878},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":869,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}